"About us - Civil Service - GOV.UK (https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/civil-service/about)\nciteturn11view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/civil-service/about\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 185\nL0: ## Navigation menu\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Menu】 Menu\nL3: ### Services and information\nL4:   * 【1†Benefits】\nL5:   * 【2†Births, death, marriages and care】\nL6:   * 【3†Business and self-employed】\nL7:   * 【4†Childcare and parenting】\nL8:   * 【5†Citizenship and living in the UK】\nL9:   * 【6†Crime, justice and the law】\nL10:   * 【7†Disabled people】\nL11:   * 【8†Driving and transport】\nL12:   * 【9†Education and learning】\nL13:   * 【10†Employing people】\nL14:   * 【11†Environment and countryside】\nL15:   * 【12†Housing and local services】\nL16:   * 【13†Money and tax】\nL17:   * 【14†Passports, travel and living abroad】\nL18:   * 【15†Visas and immigration】\nL19:   * 【16†Working, jobs and pensions】\nL20: ### Government activity\nL21: \nL22:   * 【17†Departments】\nL23: \nL24: Departments, agencies and public bodies\nL25: \nL26:   * 【18†News】\nL27: \nL28: News stories, speeches, letters and notices\nL29: \nL30:   * 【19†Guidance and regulation】\nL31: \nL32: Detailed guidance, regulations and rules\nL33: \nL34:   * 【20†Research and statistics】\nL35: \nL36: Reports, analysis and official statistics\nL37: \nL38:   * 【21†Policy papers and consultations】\nL39: \nL40: Consultations and strategy\nL41: \nL42:   * 【22†Transparency】\nL43: \nL44: Data, Freedom of Information releases and corporate reports\nL45: \nL46: Search GOV.UK × 【23†Search GOV.UK】\nL47: ### Search\nL48: \nL49: [Input]\nL50: \nL51: Search\nL52: \nL53: 【24†Civil Service】\nL54: \nL55: # About us - Civil Service\nL56: \nL57: The Civil Service serves the government of the day and supports them to develop and implement its policies as effectively as possible.\nL58: \nL59: * * *\nL60: ## Contents\nL61: \nL62:   1. 【25†Who we are】\nL63:   2. 【26†Functional model】\nL64:   3. 【27†Civil Service People Survey】\nL65:   4. 【28†Corporate information】\nL66: \nL67: Civil servants are part of your local community and all around the UK, providing essential services that help, improve and save lives. And as society changes, we are changing and modernising with it to make sure we keep delivering brilliant, efficient and effective public services for millions of people every day.\nL68: In all that we do, we uphold the 【29†Civil Service code】 and our values of integrity, honesty, objectivity, and impartiality.\nL69: \nL70: The Civil Service provides services directly to people all over the country, including:\nL71: \nL72:   * paying benefits and pensions\nL73:   * running employment services\nL74:   * running prisons\nL75:   * issuing driving licences\nL76: \nL77: We also have staff working on policy development and implementation, including analysts, project managers, lawyers and economists.\nL78: ## Who we are\nL79: \nL80: We’re politically impartial and work in central government departments, agencies, and non-departmental government bodies (NDPBs).The Civil Service does not include government ministers (who are politically appointed), members of the British Armed Forces, the police, officers of local government or NDPBs of the Houses of Parliament, employees of the National Health Service (NHS), or staff of the Royal Household.\nL81: We’re co-ordinated and managed by the Prime Minister, in his role as Minister for the Civil Service. The most senior civil servant in a department is a 【30†permanent secretary】.\nL82: \nL83: As we’re accountable to the public we need to meet the highest possible standards in all that we do. We aim to have:\nL84:   * integrity - putting the obligations of public service above personal interests\nL85:   * honesty - being truthful and open\nL86:   * objectivity - basing advice and decisions on rigorous analysis of the evidence\nL87:   * impartiality - acting solely according to the merits of the case and serving governments of different political parties equally well\nL88: \nL89: In order to make best practice the standard we are currently undergoing a period of change. This will make our service more skilled, less bureaucratic and more unified.\nL90: ### Equality and diversity\nL91: \nL92: We publish our equality and diversity statistics as part of 【31†Civil Service headline workforce information】. Click here for more information about our 【32†equality and diversity policy and how we monitor equality and diversity】.\nL93: \nL94: ## Functional model\nL95: \nL96: Government functions form a framework for collaboration across government, to support delivery of outcomes.\nL97: \nL98: For more information, see 【33†government functions】.\nL99: ## Civil Service People Survey\nL100: \nL101: The 【34†Civil Service People Survey Hub】 brings together all Civil Service People Survey reports, data, case studies and blog posts. The People Survey runs annually in over 100 organisations.\nL102: \nL103: ## Corporate information\nL104: \nL105: ### Access our information\nL106: \nL107:   * 【32†Equality and diversity】\nL108:   * 【35†Our governance】\nL109: \nL110: ### Jobs and contracts\nL111: \nL112:   * 【36†Working for the Civil Service】\nL113: \nL114: 【37†Contents】\nL115: ## Is this page useful?\nL116: \nL117:   * 【38†Maybe】\nL118:   * Yes this page is useful\nL119:   * No this page is not useful\nL120: \nL121: Thank you for your feedback\nL122: \nL123: [Button: Report a problem with this page ]\nL124: \nL125: [Input]\nL126: \nL127: ## Help us improve GOV.UK\nL128: \nL129: Do not include personal or financial information like your National Insurance number or credit card details.\nL130: \nL131: This field is for robots only. Please leave blank [Input]\nL132: \nL133: What were you doing?\nL134: \nL135: What went wrong?\nL136: \nL137: [Button: Send] [Button: Cancel]\nL138: ## Help us improve GOV.UK\nL139: \nL140: To help us improve GOV.UK, we’d like to know more about your visit today. 【39†Please fill in this survey (opens in a new tab and requires JavaScript)†www.smartsurvey.co.uk】.\nL141: \nL142: [Button: Cancel]\nL143: ## Services and information\nL144:   * 【1†Benefits】\nL145:   * 【2†Births, death, marriages and care】\nL146:   * 【3†Business and self-employed】\nL147:   * 【4†Childcare and parenting】\nL148:   * 【5†Citizenship and living in the UK】\nL149:   * 【6†Crime, justice and the law】\nL150:   * 【7†Disabled people】\nL151:   * 【8†Driving and transport】\nL152:   * 【9†Education and learning】\nL153:   * 【10†Employing people】\nL154:   * 【11†Environment and countryside】\nL155:   * 【12†Housing and local services】\nL156:   * 【13†Money and tax】\nL157:   * 【14†Passports, travel and living abroad】\nL158:   * 【15†Visas and immigration】\nL159:   * 【16†Working, jobs and pensions】\nL160: ## Government activity\nL161: \nL162:   * 【17†Departments】\nL163:   * 【18†News】\nL164:   * 【19†Guidance and regulation】\nL165:   * 【20†Research and statistics】\nL166:   * 【21†Policy papers and consultations】\nL167:   * 【22†Transparency】\nL168:   * 【40†How government works】\nL169:   * 【41†Get involved】\nL170: \nL171: * * *\nL172: ## Support links\nL173: \nL174:   * 【42†Help】\nL175:   * 【43†Privacy】\nL176:   * 【44†Cookies】\nL177:   * 【45†Accessibility statement】\nL178:   * 【46†Contact】\nL179:   * 【47†Terms and conditions】\nL180:   * 【48†Rhestr o Wasanaethau Cymraeg】\nL181:   * 【49†Government Digital Service】\nL182: All content is available under the 【50†Open Government Licence v3.0†www.nationalarchives.gov.uk】, except where otherwise stated\nL183: \nL184: 【51†© Crown copyright†www.nationalarchives.gov.uk】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaintaining the secrecy of the ballot | Electoral Commission (https://www.electoralcommission.org.uk/guidance-candidates-and-agents-uk-parliamentary-general-elections-great-britain/polling-day/who-can-support-you-polling-day/maintaining-secrecy-ballot)\nciteturn11view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.electoralcommission.org.uk/guidance-candidates-and-agents-uk-parliamentary-general-elections-great-britain/polling-day/who-can-support-you-polling-day/maintaining-secrecy-ballot\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 101\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: Please enable JavaScript in your web browser to get the best experience.\nL3: \nL4: English\nL5: \nL6:   * 【1†English】\nL7:   * 【2†Cymraeg】\nL8: \nL9: Menu\nL10: \nL11:   * 【3†Our guidance】\nL12: \nL13: 【3†Our guidance】\nL14: \nL15: We provide guidance about the process of running elections, standing as a candidate, and campaigning at an election.\nL16:     * 【4†Electoral Administrator】\nL17:     * 【5†Candidate or agent】\nL18:     * 【6†Political party】\nL19:     * 【7†Campaigner】\nL20:     * 【8†Other regulated individuals and organisations】\nL21:     * 【9†Police election SPOC (Single Point of Contact)】\nL22: \nL23:   * 【10†Voting and elections】\nL24: \nL25: 【10†Voting and elections】\nL26: \nL27: Find out everything you need to know about voting and elections, including information about upcoming elections in your area.\nL28:     * 【11†Register to vote】\nL29:     * 【12†Elections in your area】\nL30:     * 【13†Who can vote】\nL31:     * 【14†Ways to vote】\nL32:     * 【15†Voter ID】\nL33:     * 【16†Understanding political ads, leaflets and campaign messages】\nL34:     * 【17†Reporting electoral fraud】\nL35:     * 【18†Ways to get involved in democracy】\nL36: \nL37:   * 【19†Research, reports and data】\nL38: \nL39: 【19†Research, reports and data】\nL40: Part of our role includes conducting research and gathering data, and publishing reports and making recommendations about elections that take place across the UK.\nL41:     * 【20†Public attitudes towards abuse and intimidation of election candidates】\nL42:     * 【21†How different levels of donation caps could affect political parties: sensitivity analysis】\nL43:     * 【22†Our reports and data on past elections and referendums】\nL44:     * 【23†Electoral registration research】\nL45:     * 【24†Public attitudes】\nL46:     * 【25†Modernising voting: flexible voting feasibility studies】\nL47:     * 【26†Electoral fraud data】\nL48:     * 【27†Referendum question research】\nL49: \nL50:   * 【28†Political registration and regulation】\nL51: 【28†Political registration and regulation】\nL52: \nL53: We are responsible for registering political parties, publishing political finance data, and taking action if we have reason to suspect political finance law has been broken.\nL54: \nL55:     * 【29†Political party registration】\nL56:     * 【30†Financial reporting】\nL57:     * 【31†Our enforcement work】\nL58:     * 【32†Imprints】\nL59: \nL60:   * 【33†News and views】\nL61: \nL62: 【33†News and views】\nL63: Get the latest news from us, and read media handbooks for elections. We also hold consultations, and publish our responses to consultations from others.\nL64: \nL65:     * 【34†Media centre】\nL66:     * 【35†The Representation of the People Bill】\nL67:     * 【36†Our priorities for reforming elections】\nL68:     * 【37†Key correspondence】\nL69:     * 【38†Our work with the UK Parliament】\nL70:     * 【39†Our consultations】\nL71:     * 【40†Our responses to consultations】\nL72: \nL73:   * 【41†Resources】\nL74: \nL75: 【41†Resources】\nL76: Use our resources to share information about voting and democracy. Take a look at our resources for young people. Download lesson plans and activities for use in the classroom or youth groups. Share our assets and get the message out.\nL77: \nL78:     * 【42†Resources for young people】\nL79:     * 【43†Resources for educators】\nL80:     * 【44†Democratic engagement resources】\nL81: \nL82: Back\nL83: # Guidance for Candidates and Agents at UK Parliamentary general elections in Great Britain\nL84: \nL85: Print this page | 【45†Print full guidance】\nL86: ## Maintaining the secrecy of the ballot\nL87: \nL88: Anyone attending a polling station has a duty to maintain the secrecy of the ballot.【46†1】   In particular, the following information must not be disclosed:\nL89: \nL90:   * the name or electoral number of who has or has not voted\nL91:   * the number or other unique identifying mark on the ballot paper\nL92: \nL93: Anyone attending a polling station must also not try to ascertain how a voter has voted or who they are about to vote for.\nL94: A polling agent can mark off on their copy of the register of electors those voters who have applied for ballot papers. If the polling agent leaves the polling station during the hours of polling, they must leave the marked copy of the register in the polling station to ensure that secrecy requirements are not breached.\nL95: Any person found guilty of breaching the secrecy requirements can face a fine or imprisonment. In England and Wales, the fine is unlimited; in Scotland, it is up to £5,000. You can find a copy of the secrecy requirements here:\nL96: \nL97: 【47†Secrecy requirements - the poll (DOCX)】\nL98: \nL99: Last updated: 2 July 2024\nL100: ##\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn11view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.un.org/ruleoflaw/en/what-is-the-rule-of-law-archived\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.un.org/ruleoflaw/en/what-is-the-rule-of-law-archived: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn11view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.uscis.gov/citizenship/learn-about-citizenship/citizenship-and-naturalization\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.uscis.gov/citizenship/learn-about-citizenship/citizenship-and-naturalization: (403) Forbidden"
